    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d160-a1">

                <pb facs="0392" n="268" xml:id="pg268"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 152.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Tsung-li Yamên to Mr.
                            Angell.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-04-24"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 24,
                        1881</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">Semi-official.]</seg></opener>
                <p>With reference to the disturbance in the Yen-erh Hutung,<anchor n="*"
                        xml:id="d160-f1"/> the writers have to state that his excellency Ch’ung has
                    proceeded to the locality, in person, to suppress the mob. Five men have been
                    arrested and will be punished. Quiet has now been restored, and moreover a small
                    company of men have been ordered to remain there for protection. The writers
                    trust there will be no farther cause for anxiety, and write this special letter
                    for your excellency’s information.</p>
                <p>With compliments, &amp;c.,</p>
                <p>Cards of the ministers.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d160-f1" n="*" xml:id="d160-f1-content">
                <p>The street in which the American Presbyterian mission is situated.</p>
